The QR code appeared on the counter on the first Monday of November. An employee from headquarters placed a transparent acrylic stand next to my number dispenser. The stand read "Faster with Haeon Bank App" in Korean. Beneath it was a black square. I had been counting money at this window for twenty years. The newly posted square seemed to reduce my work to a single frame.

Two more shops on the first floor of our building closed this year. The news said the number of bank branches nationwide has been declining for years. The figures changed each time, but the direction never did. Our branch was cut from six counters to three. I sit at one of the remaining three. Fewer than ten customers come before noon now, year after year.

But something strange happened. People decreased, yet some people stopped leaving.

There's Park, an elderly woman who comes every Monday at 8:50. She pulls a ticket and sits waiting. She pulls a ticket even when there are no customers ahead. She believes following the order is a courtesy to the bank. When I'm free, she hands me her passbook and says, "I'd like to withdraw twenty thousand won."

Twenty thousand won takes twenty seconds at an ATM. I've never said that. I take her passbook, make an entry, count the bills, and hand them over. Meanwhile, she talks about her grandson who got into college then took a leave, about her knees, about the day her late husband first left his paycheck with me at this counter. He wore a suit that day, and it snowed, she says. I've heard that story about twelve times. Each time, the date of the snow changes by a day.

"It's good that you never change, miss."

Grandmother still calls me "miss." I'm forty-nine. I don't correct her.

On Tuesday, Mr. Kim comes—a stationery shop owner who closed his business. His request is always the same: "Check if the balance is right." On Wednesday, an elderly man whose name I never learned comes to ask about deposit rates, then leaves without signing up. At first, it bothered me that the sale didn't go through. Now, the two tangerines he sometimes brings concerns me more. They're always warm—the temperature of someone's pocket.

The branch manager said in a meeting, "Contactless transaction rates are rising. Counter staff will be reassigned sequentially." The word "reassigned" is soft. I know what direction that points. After the meeting, I stood by the window for a long time with a cup of instant coffee, looking at the QR code sign outside. In a way, it's a pretty picture. The dots and lines are precise, and one touch on a phone opens everything.

I told my daughter about it, and she laughed. "Mom, I do everything through the app. I haven't been to a bank in three years." It wasn't mean, which is why it stayed with me. I didn't know my daughter's banking habits. It was that day I learned she had never needed what I'd spent a lifetime doing.

Yet Monday always comes.

Last week, Grandmother didn't hand me her passbook. She just sat in the chair, fingering her ticket. I spoke first. "Grandmother, what would you like today?"

After a long pause, she said, "My granddaughter installed it for me. On my phone. She says I don't need to come anymore."

I kept my face composed. I've practiced that face for twenty years. "That must be convenient."

"It is," Grandmother nodded. "You just press it. But when you press it, the money goes, and nobody tells you it went. You always tell me when I withdraw twenty thousand—'Here's twenty thousand.'"

Then Grandmother pulled out her phone and showed me. Numbers appeared on the screen that I didn't recognize. She said she couldn't tell if it was really her money. I didn't know how to comfort her, so I took her passbook and made an entry. There was a transaction to record, yet I made an empty line. There's no rule for this. I couldn't print anything in that space and just left the line blank.

Grandmother put the passbook in her bag. Then she got up without withdrawing any money.

On my way home, I looked at the sign again outside the building. I pulled out my phone and pointed it at the black square. The app opened and recognized me instantly. A welcome message appeared. The name was correct. But the message wasn't directed at me.

I don't know who will pull a ticket next Monday at 8:50. The counter chair will sit empty as it always does. Sometimes I think that chair will outlast this branch longer than I will.

Monday morning, I wiped the spot in my drawer where those two warm tangerines had rested.
